======================================================================
PART 6: Proof sketch for LRC@14
======================================================================

PROVED INGREDIENTS:
  THM-381: Lonely iff observer is source in marked tournament
  THM-384: Lonely iff both adjacent gaps >= 1/14
  THM-387: Gap flow LS→LL→SL (directed, no SL→LL)
  THM-369: Sieve — primitive set covers all moduli
  HYP-1996: Only 2*Fib(11)=178 circular iso-classes for 13 runners

THE CRT QUOTIENT STRATEGY:
  14 = 2 × 7. Group 13 runners into 7 mod-7 classes.
  LRC = all 7 classes simultaneously safe.
  Each class is a 2-runner sub-problem (or 1-runner singleton).

WHAT'S NEEDED:
  1. Show that the CRT class-safe sets can't perfectly tile.
     (Their union measure is ~7*(6/7)^2 ≈ 5.3 >> 1, so massive overlap)
  2. Use the gap-race structure (THM-387) on the quotient.
  3. The quotient walk on {0,...,7} (quotient outdeg) must reach 7.
  4. Key: each class-safe set has measure ≈ (6/7)^2 ≈ 73.5%.
     The intersection of 7 such sets (measure ≈ 37.6% if independent)
     cannot be empty if the correlation is bounded.

POTENTIAL PROOF ROUTE:
  A. CORRELATION BOUND: for any two CRT classes C,D with coprime
     speed representatives, the correlation between safe_C and safe_D
     is bounded: |corr| < 1 - epsilon(n).
  B. INCLUSION-EXCLUSION: with bounded correlations and each class
     safe ≈ 73.5% of the time, the intersection is positive.
  C. CONTINUITY: positive measure implies the set is nonempty.

THE CREATIVE INSIGHT:
  The CRT quotient reduces LRC@14 to a 7-DIMENSIONAL coverage problem.
  Each dimension is a CRT class. The 'holes' in each class-safe set
  are thin strips of width 2/14 on the torus. The question is whether
  7 thin strips can cover the entire torus. Since each strip has
  width 2/14 ≈ 14.3%, and there are only 7 of them (total width < 100%),
  there must be GAPS in the coverage — and those gaps are the lonely times.

  Wait — the 7 class-UNSAFE sets each have measure ≈ (1-(6/7)^2) ≈ 26.5%.
  Their union has measure ≤ 7*26.5% = 185% >> 100%.
  So the union CAN cover [0,1). The question is whether it ALWAYS does.

  But with the CRT structure, the unsafe sets have ARITHMETIC spacing.
  They can't tile perfectly because the gcd constraints from primitivity
  force misalignment between different classes' unsafe intervals.

